{
  "result": "The lighthouse stood on a cliff so old that the sea had learned to whisper around it. Every evening, Elias climbed the ninety-seven iron steps with a lantern in his left hand and a letter in his right. The letter was always the same, written in blue ink by a woman he had never met. She wrote about rain, about bread, about children who asked questions he could not answer. He read the words aloud while the gulls settled on the railing, pretending not to listen. Then he folded the page carefully and placed it inside the hollow of the great lens. It was not a practical place for paper, but Elias believed secrets belonged where light could find them. On clear nights the beam swept across the water and touched distant ships, islands, and sleeping towns. The sailors waved, thinking someone on shore was waving back. Elias always waved, too, with both arms, enormous and ridiculous. He did this because loneliness, he had learned, grows smaller when it is shared without permission. The sea did not care about any of this, which was why he loved it. It arrived and departed without explanation, taking with it the names of drowned heroes and forgotten storms. Still, every morning Elias swept the floor, trimmed the wick, and waited for the post. One letter would come, he was certain, signed not with a stranger's name but with his own. Until then, he would keep the light burning. And let the horizon answer.",
